const lessonSlides = {"lessons":[{"id":"u1-w01-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Plan a badge-cost program","prepare":"Whiteboard or paper; chapter open. No Python installation needed for this lesson.","slides":[{"title":"Plan a badge-cost program","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 1","Explain the problem before writing code."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Introduce the week: plan, run, trace/test, then apply. Ask learners to explain what a successful badge calculator would do. This lesson is diagnostic and formative.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"What does a program need?","body":["A club sells badges for $3 each.","Think alone: what input do we need?","Compare with a partner: what should four badges cost?","Share your method, then try six badges."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Allow 45 seconds of silent thinking, one minute comparing, then invite two explanations. Expected: quantity is input, 4 × 3 = $12, 6 × 3 = $18. Accept diagrams or spoken explanations. If learners add 3 to the quantity, use equal groups to model the price per badge.","strategy":"Think, pair, share"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["I can identify input, processing and output.","I can write calculation steps in order.","I can predict a result before testing."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Have learners restate one criterion in their own words. Distinguish the variable quantity from the stated fixed price. Return to these criteria at the exit check.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"The badge requirement","body":["Display the cost for a valid badge quantity.","Each badge costs $3.","Assume a whole-number quantity of zero or more.","Input validation is taught later."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Think aloud as you underline quantity, circle the price and identify the required output. Model why zero is allowed by the stated requirement. Ask everyone to identify one valid input before continuing. If several learners choose a negative quantity, revisit the assumption. Validation comes later.","strategy":"Teacher modelling"},{"title":"A worked example: four badges","table":[["Stage","Badge example"],["Input","Quantity: 4"],["Processing","4 × 3 = 12"],["Output","Total: $12"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Model input 4, processing 4 × 3 and output Total: $12, explaining each decision. Then learners create the same three rows for quantity 6 without copying the numbers. Ask everyone to show their processing row. Expected $18. If they write 6 + 3, model equal groups again before independent practice.","strategy":"Worked example and guided practice"},{"title":"An algorithm for any quantity","body":["Write the steps without looking at the example.","Trace your steps for 0, 1 and 6 badges.","Explain your sequence to a partner.","Revise any step your partner cannot follow."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Allow six minutes independently, three minutes of explanation in pairs, then three minutes to revise. Expected sequence: read quantity, multiply by 3, display total. Expected totals $0, $3, $18. Support: offer the three stage names Input, Processing, Output. Stretch: explain why displaying total before assigning it fails. Keep individual plans as evidence.","strategy":"Independent practice and peer explanation"},{"title":"Which rule matches the requirement?","body":["For six badges, choose a rule on paper.","A: quantity + 3     B: quantity × 3","Show your choice together.","Explain what the other rule gets wrong."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect a simultaneous response before discussion so every learner commits. Correct B gives $18. A gives $9 and adds a price once rather than charging for each badge. If several choose A, use a drawing of six groups of $3 and ask a fresh check for two badges before moving on.","strategy":"All-student misconception check"},{"title":"Exit check: seven badges","body":["Name the input.","State the processing step.","Give the expected displayed total.","Explain why an expected result is useful."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect an individual written response without partner help. Expected input quantity 7, processing multiply by 3, Total: $21. An independent expectation checks correctness. Sort responses into ready, input/processing confusion and calculation support, and use these groups to plan the next lesson starter.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Run your first Python script","prepare":"School-approved Python 3 editor/IDE, writable practice folder and projector. Check the school’s Run command before class.","slides":[{"title":"Run your first Python script","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 2","Turn the badge algorithm into a saved program."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Use the school-approved environment. Do not ask learners to install software or change school settings during the lesson.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"Retrieve the badge plan","body":["Close your notes for one minute.","Name the input and calculation step.","Predict the cost of six badges.","Compare with your notes and correct one detail."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Use one minute of individual retrieval, then compare and correct. Expected quantity, quantity × 3, $18. Ask everyone to show the calculation. If confusion remains between input and processing, briefly return to the three-row badge example before opening the editor.","strategy":"Retrieval practice"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Save and run a Python script.","Enter suitable data when prompted.","Compare actual output with a prediction."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success: the learner saves badge_cost.py, runs it independently, identifies where to enter the quantity and records a matching prediction/result.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"From demonstration to your own file","body":["Watch the teacher save badge_cost.py.","Point out the editor and output area.","Create your own file in this week’s folder.","Show your partner where Save and Run are."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Demonstrate in the school-approved editor for three minutes, then pause for learners to repeat each step. Ask partners to point to controls on their own screens, without taking over. Check everyone has a saved .py file before continuing. Save stores changes and Run executes them. Provide a prepared folder or printed instructions for setup support. Avoid filenames that shadow modules, such as random.py.","strategy":"Demonstration with guided rehearsal"},{"title":"The badge script","code":"BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input(\"Number of badges: \"))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","minutes":10,"notes":"Read the code top to bottom and connect each line to the paper plan. Ask learners to point to the line that reads input before you explain it. input returns text and int converts suitable whole-number text. Pause before the total line and elicit a prediction for quantity 4. print displays the result. Uppercase BADGE_PRICE signals an intended fixed value, but Python does not enforce it. Model the reasoning rather than only typing.","strategy":"Code reading and teacher think-aloud"},{"title":"A prediction before each run","body":["Write your prediction for four badges.","Run the saved script and enter 4.","Compare expected and actual output with a partner.","Repeat for 1 and 0, predicting each time."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Use individual predictions before learners work in pairs. Driver runs the file and navigator checks the prediction and explains a line, then swap for the next quantity. Expected $12, $3, $0. If a result differs, inspect the saved file and input before changing code. Each learner retains a prediction/result record. Use suitable inputs only.","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate"},{"title":"One change, a new prediction","body":["Change BADGE_PRICE from 3 to 5.","Predict four badges before running.","Explain why the input line stays the same.","Return the price to 3 after checking."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Expected $20. Ask all learners to write why quantity still needs input while the fixed price changes. If the output stays $12, check saving and which file is running. Support: highlight the price line. Stretch: predict a new price and quantity before testing. Keep the original file structure.","strategy":"Controlled variation and explanation"},{"title":"Exit check: run independently","body":["Show your saved Python file.","Run it for two badges at $3 each.","State the expected output before running.","Explain the difference between Save and Run."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Each learner saves and runs their own file for quantity 2 at price 3, predicting Total: $6 first. Ask for a spoken or written explanation of Save versus Run. Record setup difficulties separately from conceptual errors. Start the next lesson with a short demonstration for learners who need environment support.","strategy":"Independent performance check"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Trace, modify and test a sequence","prepare":"Working badge_cost.py at price 3; paper trace table or worksheet; Python editor.","slides":[{"title":"Trace, modify and test","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 3","Explain what changes as each instruction runs."],"minutes":2,"notes":"Learners should already be able to run the badge script. Keep a clean copy available so setup errors do not consume the tracing lesson.","strategy":"Learning intention"},{"title":"Retrieve the script","code":"BADGE_PRICE = 3\nquantity = int(input(\"Number of badges: \"))\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","body":["Predict the output for quantity 5."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Learners predict Total: $15 individually before running. Ask them to annotate which line reads input, which calculates and which displays. Compare annotations in pairs. If they treat print as the calculation, trace the total assignment explicitly before moving on.","strategy":"Retrieval and code annotation"},{"title":"Today’s goal","body":["Trace variable values in execution order.","Modify a calculation to match a requirement.","Record expected and actual results."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Success: complete a trace for a new input, add a fixed charge correctly, and explain at least one meaningful test. A trace records state after each instruction, not just the final answer.","strategy":"Success criteria"},{"title":"Trace four badges","table":[["After instruction","Value known"],["BADGE_PRICE = 3","BADGE_PRICE is 3"],["Read quantity 4","quantity is 4"],["Calculate total","total is 12"],["Display total","Output: Total: $12"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Think aloud through the model for quantity 4. Cover the model or move off this slide and ask learners to trace quantity 2 using blank rows. Expected price 3, quantity 2, total 6 and output Total: $6. total has no assigned value before its calculation. Support: retain instruction labels. Stretch: explain which values remain unchanged at each line. Check every learner’s total row before the next task.","strategy":"Worked trace and faded support"},{"title":"Add a fixed packaging charge","code":"PACKAGING = 2\ntotal = quantity * BADGE_PRICE + PACKAGING","body":["The $2 charge applies to every order, including zero badges.","Predict the totals for quantities 0 and 3."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Model adding PACKAGING = 2 above the total calculation. Learners explain in pairs why packaging is added once, then independently predict quantity 0 ($2) and 3 ($11). Check answers before implementation. Contrast the stated zero-order rule with possible alternative requirements without changing this task. Multiplication precedes addition here.","strategy":"Guided prediction and code modification"},{"title":"Test the modified program","table":[["Quantity","Expected","Actual"],["0","$2","Record your run"],["1","$5","Record your run"],["3","$11","Record your run"]],"minutes":12,"notes":"Driver implements and runs the modification. Navigator checks the requirement and compares expected with actual output. Swap roles after the first case. Every learner keeps their own test record. Expected $2, $5, $11. Keep expected values unchanged when actual differs. Support: provide the correct input and output lines. Stretch: find a quantity where an incorrect packaging-per-item calculation accidentally passes (quantity 1).","strategy":"Paired testing with role changes"},{"title":"Two rules, different costs","body":["For quantity 3, which rule charges packaging once?","A: quantity × 3 + 2     B: quantity × (3 + 2)","Choose alone, then explain to a partner.","Why does a test with quantity 1 miss the error?"],"minutes":5,"notes":"Take simultaneous A/B responses before partner discussion. A gives $11 and B gives $15. Both are syntactically valid, so B is a logic error for this requirement. Quantity 1 produces $5 for both and cannot distinguish them. If several choose B, model one packaging charge separately and recheck quantity 2: $8 versus $10. A syntax error prevents Python parsing the instructions.","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis"},{"title":"Exit check: a changed order","body":["Badges cost $3 each. Packaging is $2 per order.","Predict the cost of six badges.","Give the expected result for quantity 0.","Explain which test could reveal a packaging error."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect individual responses: quantity 6 gives $20 and quantity 0 gives $2. A zero test exposes the packaging-per-item fault because it gives $0. Look for a requirement-based explanation and an independent expected result. Use errors to choose a brief trace or fixed-charge starter next lesson.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket"}]},{"id":"u1-w01-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Build a book-cover calculator","prepare":"Python editor, a new cover_cost.py file and a test-record template. Keep the badge example available as a reference.","slides":[{"title":"Build a book-cover calculator","body":["Unit 1 · Week 1 · Lesson 4","Plan, implement, test and explain a new problem."],"minutes":2,"notes":"This is formative transfer practice, not an additional summative assessment. Let learners use their earlier notes while making their own design and test decisions.","strategy":"Learning intention and transfer"},{"title":"Retrieve a useful test habit","body":["Write why we predict before running.","Explain a fixed charge using the badge example.","Compare explanations with a partner.","Add one detail that makes your answer clearer."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Allow one minute of writing, two minutes comparing, then two minutes sharing corrections. Expected: an independent prediction checks correctness, a fixed charge applies once per order and a trace finds the first wrong value. Invite learners to use a small numerical example if language is a barrier.","strategy":"Retrieval with peer explanation"},{"title":"The book-cover requirement","body":["Each book cover costs $2.","Every order has a $4 handling charge.","The handling charge also applies to a zero-cover order.","Assume the quantity is a whole number of zero or more."],"minutes":6,"notes":"Learners underline the input, circle the two prices and mark the zero-order rule in their own notes. Ask everyone to predict whether quantity 0 costs $0 or $4 and explain. Correct $4 under this requirement. Success means an IPO plan, readable saved script, three predicted tests and an explanation of a line. Validation comes later.","strategy":"Requirement annotation"},{"title":"Design before coding","body":["Draft your own input, processing and output plan.","Write the calculation in words.","Ask a partner to follow the steps for two covers.","Revise your plan before coding."],"minutes":10,"notes":"Five minutes independent design, three minutes partner walkthrough, two minutes revision. Expected quantity × 2 + 4, so two covers cost $8. Partners ask questions without writing the solution for each other. Support: offer a blank IPO frame. Stretch: explain how a change to handling would affect every order. Suggested names COVER_PRICE and HANDLING.","strategy":"Independent design and peer critique"},{"title":"Expected results come from the rule","table":[["Covers","Your working","Expected total"],["0","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"],["1","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"],["5","Calculate on paper","Record prediction"]],"minutes":10,"notes":"Learners calculate all three cases individually before any program runs. Expected 0 × 2 + 4 = $4, 1 × 2 + 4 = $6 and 5 × 2 + 4 = $14. Ask for working, then compare in pairs. Keep answers in teacher notes so the slide does not give away the task. Support: model a different quantity, 2 gives $8. Stretch: find a test that distinguishes adding handling once from adding it per cover.","strategy":"Independent prediction with reduced support"},{"title":"Your own working calculator","body":["Create and save cover_cost.py.","Use your plan to write the calculation.","Display a labelled result and run your tests.","Record actual results beside your predictions."],"minutes":12,"notes":"Allow independent work, with short teacher conferences. Offer the badge example or a partial file only to learners who need it, then ask them to explain the changed lines. Reference: COVER_PRICE = 2; HANDLING = 4; quantity = int(input('Covers: ')); total = quantity * COVER_PRICE + HANDLING; print(f'Total: ${total}'). Stretch: change one fixed value, design a fresh test and justify it. Do not introduce input validation in this task.","strategy":"Independent practice with optional scaffolds"},{"title":"Feedback that leads to a revision","body":["Show a partner one prediction and actual result.","Explain why handling is added once.","Partner: ask one question about the evidence.","Revise one detail and name a limitation."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Each learner explains while the partner checks against the requirement. Expected test results $4, $6, $14. Feedback should identify a specific match or mismatch, not just say good work. Learners revise their own code or explanation. A suitable limitation is that invalid text and negative quantities are not handled. Keep the original expectation if a run fails.","strategy":"Peer feedback and self-explanation"},{"title":"Exit check: a fresh quantity","body":["Predict three covers without running the code.","Explain one input, processing or output line.","Which check helped you find or avoid an error?","Save your script and test evidence."],"minutes":5,"notes":"Collect individual responses before showing the answer: $10. Ask for a specific check such as predicting zero, tracing a total or comparing an actual result. Use responses to identify learners needing setup, arithmetic or tracing support before next week’s types and operators. The task remains formative.","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection"}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Values, types and input","chapter":"values","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#values","prepare":"Approved Python 3 editor, paper for type sorting and a saved Week 1 badge script.","slides":[{"title":"Values, types and input","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain that learners will choose a type by meaning, convert suitable input and justify a prediction. This is formative practice.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 1","Choose a type that fits what a value means."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the badge program","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Allow one minute without notes, then compare with a partner. Expected quantity is input, quantity × 3 is processing, print displays output. input returns text and int converts suitable text. If learners think input returns a number, demonstrate type(input(...)) with 4 before continuing.","body":["Without notes, name the badge program’s input.","Which line calculates the cost?","Why did we use int(input(...))?","Compare with a partner, then correct your explanation."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Four useful Python types","strategy":"Type sorting and explanation","notes":"Model the first row, then learners suggest another example for each type. A count is int, a measurement can be float, an identifier stays str and a two-state value is bool. Capitalisation matters in True and False. Avoid presenting float as an exact decimal representation.","table":[["Type","Example","Meaning"],["int","24","Count of badges"],["float","2.5","Length in metres"],["str","\"0042\"","Identifier"],["bool","True","Two-state value"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"The same digits can mean different things","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided prediction","notes":"Think aloud: the first line joins strings and the second adds integers. Expected 123 then 15. Learners predict first, run, then annotate which values change type. input always returns str here. int(\"12.5\") raises ValueError while float(\"12.5\") gives 12.5. Demonstrate the failure in a separate interpreter, explaining that error handling comes later.","body":["Predict the first two outputs before running.","Explain why they differ."],"code":"print(\"12\" + \"3\")\nprint(int(\"12\") + int(\"3\"))\nmeasurement = float(\"12.5\")","minutes":10},{"title":"A type depends on its purpose","strategy":"Think, pair, share","notes":"Two minutes individually choosing types, three comparing, then share justifications. Expected locker label str, student count int, temperature float and consent flag bool. A number-looking identifier does not require arithmetic. Keep a label such as 0042 as a string to preserve its zeros. Support: supply the four type names. Stretch: explain why converting an identifier may lose information.","body":["Choose a type for each value and explain why.","Locker label \"0042\" and student count 28.","Temperature 18.5 and permission True.","Compare with a partner before changing your answers."],"minutes":10},{"title":"A measurement calculator","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Learners predict for length 2.5 and width 4: Area: 10.0, then run using suitable numeric input. Driver runs and navigator explains conversions, then swap for 3 and 2: 6.0. Each learner records predictions and results. Support: identify which lines read, calculate and display. Stretch: explain why int(input(...)) would reject the text 2.5.","body":["Predict for length 2.5 and width 4.","Run, compare and explain one conversion."],"code":"length = float(input(\"Length: \"))\nwidth = float(input(\"Width: \"))\narea = length * width\nprint(f\"Area: {area}\")","minutes":12},{"title":"Which conversion fits?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Collect A/B/C responses simultaneously. Correct B preserves 0042. A produces 42 and C produces 42.0. If several choose A, revisit identifier versus quantity and recheck a phone extension with a leading zero. Do not imply bool(\"False\") parses a Boolean word; non-empty strings are truthy, which is beyond today’s input task.","body":["An identifier must keep its leading zeros.","A: int(\"0042\")     B: \"0042\"     C: float(\"0042\")","Choose alone, then justify your answer.","What information could conversion remove?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a type and a reason","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected str for 0075, float for 1.75 metres, int for 6 items and bool for an on/off flag. \"6\" + \"2\" gives \"62\" and int(\"6\") + 2 gives 8. Collect without partner help. Use errors to group a brief type/conversion refresher next lesson.","body":["Choose types for label \"0075\" and length 1.75.","Choose types for a count and an on/off flag.","Predict \"6\" + \"2\" and int(\"6\") + 2.","Give a reason for one choice."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Operators and changing values","chapter":"values","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#values","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace tables and counters or a sketch of objects in groups.","slides":[{"title":"Operators and changing values","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Link to Week 1 calculations. Success means predict arithmetic, explain precedence, trace an update and interpret quotient/remainder for non-negative quantities.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 2","Predict calculations before you run them."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: text or number?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"Expected \"45\", 9 and float for a fractional measurement. Give one minute individually, then compare explanations. If string concatenation still causes confusion, show the two expressions side by side before introducing additional operators.","body":["Predict \"4\" + \"5\" without running it.","Predict int(\"4\") + 5.","Which type suits a measurement with a fraction?","Explain one answer to a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Arithmetic operators","strategy":"Worked examples","notes":"Model the first four operators briefly, then use counters for 17 objects packed four per group. / returns a float for these operands, so 17 / 4 is 4.25. // floors the quotient, giving 4, and % gives remainder 1. Restrict grouping tasks to non-negative integers and positive group sizes. A zero divisor raises ZeroDivisionError.","table":[["Operator","Example","Result"],["+ and -","7 + 2, 7 - 2","9, 5"],["* and /","3 * 4, 17 / 4","12, 4.25"],["//","17 // 4","4 full groups"],["%","17 % 4","1 remaining"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Order changes the result","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided practice","notes":"Model multiplication before addition for 2 + 3 * 4 = 14. Parentheses first gives (2 + 3) * 4 = 20. Learners then independently predict 18 - 6 / 3 = 16.0. Multiplication and division share precedence and evaluate left to right here. Check all responses before moving on. Support: underline the first operation.","body":["2 + 3 * 4 gives 14.","(2 + 3) * 4 gives 20.","Predict 18 - 6 / 3 and show your working.","Compare the first operation with a partner."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Assignment updates the stored value","strategy":"Worked trace and faded support","notes":"Model count = 4, then the right side count + 1 uses the old 4 and assigns 5, then multiplying by 2 assigns 10. Learners annotate the value after each line and predict print output 10. Repeat independently from count 7, giving 7, 8, 16. = assigns and == compares. Python assignment is not a mathematical claim that count equals count plus one.","body":["Write the value of count after each assignment.","Try again with a starting value of 7."],"code":"count = 4\ncount = count + 1\ncount = count * 2\nprint(count)","minutes":10},{"title":"Cones packed in boxes","strategy":"Paired coding with role changes","notes":"For 29 cones and six per box, expected full boxes 4 and remaining 5. Learners complete and run the code, then swap driver/navigator roles for tests 0, 6 and 7. Expected (0,0), (1,0), (1,1). Each learner keeps a test record. Support: give the operator meanings. Stretch: verify full_boxes * BOX_SIZE + remaining equals cones.","body":["Replace each __ with the correct operator.","Predict and test for 0, 6 and 7 cones."],"code":"cones = 29\nBOX_SIZE = 6\nfull_boxes = cones __ BOX_SIZE\nremaining = cones __ BOX_SIZE\nprint(full_boxes, remaining)","minutes":12},{"title":"Division gives different information","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis","notes":"Correct B is 2 full boxes. A gives 2.3333333333333335 and C gives remainder 2. Ask for simultaneous responses, then an explanation. If learners confuse quotient and remainder, draw two groups of six and circle the two leftovers. Recheck with 13 cones: quotient 2, remainder 1.","body":["Fourteen cones go into boxes of six.","Which expression gives the number of full boxes?","A: 14 / 6     B: 14 // 6     C: 14 % 6","Explain what each result tells you."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: predict and justify","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected 3 + 2 * 5 = 13, (3 + 2) * 5 = 25, 23 // 5 = 4 and 23 % 5 = 3. count = 8 then count = count + 1 gives 9. Collect working rather than output copied from Python. Use errors to revisit precedence or grouping.","body":["Predict 3 + 2 * 5 and (3 + 2) * 5.","Find 23 // 5 and 23 % 5.","Trace count = 8, then count = count + 1.","Explain one answer using the operation’s meaning."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Binary, decimal and hexadecimal","chapter":"representation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#representation","prepare":"Paper place-value grids, Python editor for checking after hand calculations.","slides":[{"title":"Binary and hexadecimal","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Explain that the base changes notation while the value stays the same. Success means hand conversions of small non-negative integers and a second-method check.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 3","Use place values to explain the same number in different bases."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: decimal place value","strategy":"Think, pair, share","notes":"Expected 247 = 2 × 100 + 4 × 10 + 7 × 1. The left position is ten times the next position to the right. Let learners explain with a place-value grid. This bridge prepares doubling for binary without assuming prior binary knowledge.","body":["Write 247 as hundreds, tens and ones.","Explain what the 4 contributes.","How does a position change as you move left?","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Binary place values","strategy":"Worked example and guided practice","notes":"Binary uses only 0 and 1, with each position twice the value to its right. Model 10110₂ by summing 16 + 4 + 2 = 22 decimal. Read the table from left to right. Then learners use the same headings for 11001₂: 16 + 8 + 1 = 25. Support: circle positions with 1. Ask all learners for the decimal result.","table":[["Place","16","8","4","2","1"],["Digit","1","0","1","1","0"],["Contribution","16","0","4","2","0"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Decimal 22 written in binary","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and faded support","notes":"Model choosing 16 from 22, leaving 6, skipping 8, choosing 4 then 2, leaving zero. Result 10110₂. Learners convert decimal 13 independently: 01101₂ or 1101₂. Leading zeros preserve value. Support: provide place headings 16,8,4,2,1. Stretch: convert 31 and explain why adding 1 needs a new position.","body":["Choose place values that add to 22: 16 + 4 + 2.","Write 1 for chosen places and 0 for the others.","22 decimal becomes 10110 in binary.","Now convert decimal 13 and check by adding."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Hexadecimal uses sixteen digits","strategy":"Worked example and paired explanation","notes":"Explain digits 0–9 then A=10 through F=15. For 2F hexadecimal, 2 × 16 + 15 = 47 decimal. Each hex digit maps to four bits: 2 is 0010 and F is 1111, giving 00101111. Ask pairs to explain why F is one digit rather than a variable. A bit is a binary digit, and eight bits form a byte.","table":[["Hex digit","Decimal value","Four binary digits"],["2","2","0010"],["A","10","1010"],["F","15","1111"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Conversion practice before Python","strategy":"Independent practice and peer checking","notes":"Allow seven minutes individually, three comparing working, then two using Python. Expected 11001₂ = 25; 3C₁₆ = 60 and 00111100₂; decimal 37 = 100101₂ and 25₁₆. Python bin(37), hex(37), int(\"100101\",2), int(\"25\",16) verify. Support: supply place grids and A–F values. Stretch: compare two methods for converting 3C. Do not accept Python output alone as hand-working evidence.","body":["Convert binary 11001 to decimal.","Convert hexadecimal 3C to decimal and eight-bit binary.","Convert decimal 37 to binary and hexadecimal.","Show working, compare, then check with Python."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Leading zeros and number bases","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Correct B: both equal decimal 5. Leading zeros change written width, not value. A common misconception is treating the digits 101 as decimal one hundred and one. If several choose A, return to place values 4,2,1, then recheck binary 0011 versus 11. Always state the base when notation could be ambiguous.","body":["Compare binary 00101 and binary 101.","A: different values     B: the same value","Choose alone and justify with place values.","How many bits form one byte?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: one value, three notations","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected decimal 26 is binary 11010 and hexadecimal 1A. Working: 16 + 8 + 2 and 1 × 16 + 10. A–F are hexadecimal digits for 10–15. Collect individual work. Use errors to decide whether to revisit binary positions or hexadecimal digit values in Lesson 4.","body":["Write decimal 26 in binary and hexadecimal.","Show a calculation that checks each answer.","What does A mean as a hexadecimal digit?","Explain why the value stays the same."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w02-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Characters, code points and bytes","chapter":"representation","source":"https://edugenio.github.io/cs-atar-textbook/#representation","prepare":"Python 3 editor capable of UTF-8, paper matching cards and Week 2 conversion notes.","slides":[{"title":"Characters, code points and bytes","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means distinguish a character, a Unicode code point and encoded bytes. Learners revisit base conversion while investigating text representation. Keep examples to A and é to avoid implying all visible characters use exactly one code point.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 2 · Lesson 4","Explain how a character connects to a number and encoded bytes."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the number 65","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair explanation","notes":"Expected decimal 65 is binary 1000001 or 01000001 as eight bits, and hexadecimal 41. Working 64 + 1 and 4 × 16 + 1. Allow silent working before pairs compare. If needed, model the 64 position. Use this same value for the character A next.","body":["Write decimal 65 in binary and hexadecimal.","Show place-value working for one conversion.","Compare with a partner.","What changes when we write a number in another base?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"A character and its code point","strategy":"Worked example and matching","notes":"Explain Unicode assigns A code point 65, written U+0041 in hexadecimal. These are representations of the code-point number. UTF-8 encodes A as the single byte 41 hexadecimal. Learners match the rows to character, number and bytes. A code point identifies a character in this example; some visible characters combine multiple code points.","table":[["Representation","Example for A"],["Character","A"],["Code point","65 decimal = U+0041"],["Code-point number in binary","1000001"],["UTF-8 encoded byte","41 hexadecimal"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Python can inspect a code point","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Predict 65, 0b1000001, 0x41 and A, then run. ord returns a single character’s code point, chr maps a code point back to a character. bin and hex return strings with notation prefixes. Ask learners to annotate each output as number, notation or character. This code does not show encoded bytes yet.","body":["Predict each line of output before running.","Which line returns the character?"],"code":"point = ord(\"A\")\nprint(point)\nprint(bin(point))\nprint(hex(point))\nprint(chr(point))","minutes":10},{"title":"An encoding determines the bytes","strategy":"Teacher modelling and contrast","notes":"Show A: ord 65, UTF-8 hex 41, byte length 1. Show é: ord 233 (U+00E9), UTF-8 hex c3a9, byte length 2. Run the snippet with A, then change to the single precomposed é character shown. len(encoded) counts bytes. .hex() displays those bytes in hex. Explain that a code-point number is different from the encoded byte sequence. Do not generalise one visible character to one byte.","body":["Predict whether é uses the same byte count as A.","Run and compare the two examples."],"code":"text = \"é\"\nencoded = text.encode(\"utf-8\")\nprint(ord(text))\nprint(encoded.hex())\nprint(len(encoded))","minutes":10},{"title":"A representation record","strategy":"Paired investigation with role changes","notes":"Learners complete A then swap roles for precomposed é. Expected A: code point 65/U+0041, UTF-8 41, one byte. é: 233/U+00E9, UTF-8 c3a9, two bytes. Navigator explains which column comes from ord and which from encode. Each learner records an explanation. Support: use the previous snippet. Stretch: explain why decimal 233 alone does not identify a file’s byte sequence without an encoding.","table":[["Character","Code point","UTF-8 hex","Byte count"],["A","Predict, check","Predict, check","Predict, check"],["é","Predict, check","Predict, check","Predict, check"]],"minutes":12},{"title":"How many bytes does a character use?","strategy":"Hinge question and peer explanation","notes":"Correct B. é has code point 233 but uses two UTF-8 bytes c3 a9. A uses one. Collect simultaneous responses, then ask pairs to explain using the record. If several choose A, point to the code-point and encoded-byte columns and re-run len(encoded). A byte is eight bits, but an encoded character can occupy several bytes.","body":["“Every Unicode character takes one byte in UTF-8.”","A: agree     B: disagree","Choose alone, then use A and é as evidence.","What information does a code point identify?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: explain the distinction","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"Expected A has decimal code point 65 and U+0041, encoded as one UTF-8 byte 41. é uses two UTF-8 bytes. Unicode provides code points and UTF-8 supplies encoding rules. Learners explain in their own words and identify a check that helped. Use remaining base-conversion or encoding confusion for a short retrieval starter before Week 3 selection.","body":["Explain character, code point and encoded bytes.","Give A’s code point in decimal and hexadecimal.","Use é to challenge the one-byte claim.","Which prediction or check changed your understanding?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Comparisons and two-way decisions","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace grids and the selection chapter.","slides":[{"title":"Comparisons and two-way decisions","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: predict a Boolean comparison, trace an if/else branch and justify a boundary test. Connect selection to Week 1 sequence. This lesson assumes suitable non-negative numeric input.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 1","Use a condition to choose which instructions run."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: assignment and types","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"One minute without notes, then compare. Expected count becomes 5 and input returns str. = assigns while == compares. Ask everyone to identify which symbol tests equality. If confusion remains, show count = 4 followed by count == 4, which evaluates True without updating count.","body":["Trace count = 4, then count = count + 1.","What type does input() return?","What does = do in Python?","Compare your answers before opening your notes."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Comparisons produce Boolean results","strategy":"Worked examples and guided prediction","notes":"Model the first two rows, then learners explain why 50 >= 50 is True while 50 > 50 is False. Cover the result column or use a fresh example: 12 \u003c= 12 is True. = assigns, == compares. True and False have capital initial letters in Python.","table":[["Comparison","Meaning","Example result"],["== and !=","Equal / not equal","5 == 5 is True"],["\u003c and >","Less / greater than","5 > 8 is False"],["\u003c=","Less than or equal","5 \u003c= 5 is True"],[">=","Greater than or equal","50 >= 50 is True"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"A delivery rule with a boundary","strategy":"Think, pair, share","notes":"Read the rule together, then allow two minutes of silent predictions. Expected delivery charges: 35 gives $5, 50 gives $0, 80 gives $0. Invite pairs to explain why the boundary belongs to the free-delivery branch. Assume order total is a valid number of zero or more. This is a teaching example, not a robust payment application.","body":["Orders of $50 or more have free delivery.","Smaller orders have a $5 delivery charge.","Predict the charge for totals 35, 50 and 80.","Explain which comparison matches “$50 or more”."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Two branches, one chosen path","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and code annotation","notes":"Trace order_total 35: condition False, else assigns 5, final print runs and displays Delivery: $5.00. Repeat for 50: True, if assigns 0, output Delivery: $0.00. Colon introduces a suite and indentation groups its instructions. Ask learners to mark the two branches and circle the final print outside them. Invalid input handling comes later.","body":["Trace once for 35, then for 50.","Which line runs after either branch?"],"code":"order_total = float(input(\"Order total: \"))\nif order_total >= 50:\n    delivery = 0\nelse:\n    delivery = 5\nprint(f\"Delivery: ${delivery:.2f}\")","minutes":10},{"title":"A trace before each run","strategy":"Paired prediction, run and explanation","notes":"Driver enters an input while navigator checks the prior prediction and explains the selected branch. Swap after the first run. Expected 49.99 False/else/$5.00; 50 True/if/$0.00; 50.01 True/if/$0.00. Every learner records their own trace. Support: supply condition and branch headings. Stretch: explain which test detects > instead of >=.","table":[["Order total","Condition","Branch and charge"],["49.99","Predict first","Trace, then run"],["50.00","Predict first","Trace, then run"],["50.01","Predict first","Trace, then run"]],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which condition includes the boundary?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Take simultaneous responses. Correct B is >= 50. A excludes exactly 50 and C excludes all larger orders. If several choose A, mark 50 on a number line and retest with exactly 50 before moving on. Ask a learner choosing B to explain the equality part.","body":["Which condition matches “at least 50”?","A: total > 50     B: total >= 50     C: total == 50","Choose alone and show your answer together.","Name an input that exposes each wrong choice."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a new decision","strategy":"Independent transfer check","notes":"Museum rule: under 16 costs $8, otherwise $12. Expected age 15 gives $8, 16 gives $12, 17 gives $12. Correct condition age \u003c 16 for the cheaper branch. Collect the condition and one trace, without partner help. Revisit equality or branch tracing next lesson if needed.","body":["A museum charges $8 for ages under 16.","Everyone else pays $12.","Write the condition for the cheaper branch.","Predict prices at ages 15, 16 and 17."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Combining Boolean conditions","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor, paper response cards and a four-row truth-table frame.","slides":[{"title":"Combining Boolean conditions","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: explain and/or/not, evaluate a combined rule and use parentheses to make grouping explicit. Use supplied Boolean values rather than parsing yes/no input.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 2","Explain when a combined condition becomes True."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the equality boundary","strategy":"Individual retrieval with correction","notes":"Expected False, True and True. Ask for one minute of individual work, then paired explanations. If > and >= remain confused, return to the number-line boundary before combining conditions.","body":["Predict 50 > 50 and 50 >= 50.","Predict 15 \u003c 16.","Explain one answer to a partner.","Correct any disagreement using the comparison’s meaning."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Both, either and the opposite","strategy":"Worked truth table","notes":"Model the first row and ask learners to predict the remaining rows before revealing them or checking with Python. and requires both True, or needs at least one True and includes both, not reverses a Boolean. Here P and Q stand for Boolean values. A common error is interpreting or as exactly one.","table":[["P","Q","P and Q","P or Q"],["True","True","True","True"],["True","False","False","True"],["False","True","False","True"],["False","False","False","False"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"An eligibility rule","strategy":"Teacher modelling and paired variation","notes":"Rule: eligibility requires age at least 16 AND permission. With supplied values age 16 and has_permission False, result is False. Ask learners to predict changing permission to True, then age to 15. Expected True then False. has_permission is already bool. bool(\"False\") is True because the string is non-empty, so do not use it to parse input.","body":["Predict before running.","Change one value, then explain the new result."],"code":"age = 16\nhas_permission = False\neligible = age >= 16 and has_permission\nprint(eligible)","minutes":10},{"title":"Grouping changes which rule you test","strategy":"Guided prediction and peer explanation","notes":"Use the stated values to evaluate both expressions. A is True because and binds more tightly than or. B is False because the parenthesised or is True but not suspended is False. Explain Python Boolean operator precedence: not, then and, then or. Comparisons here evaluate before these Boolean combinations. Ask learners to annotate sub-results. If confusion remains, replace each sub-expression with True/False on paper.","body":["member = True, guest = False, suspended = True","A: member or guest and not suspended","B: (member or guest) and not suspended","Predict each result and explain the grouping."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Testing every Boolean combination","strategy":"Paired investigation with role changes","notes":"Use supplied booleans rather than text input. Learners implement allowed = (member or guest) and not suspended and choose cases before running. Expected True/False/False gives True; False/True/False gives True; True/True/True gives False; False/False/False gives False. These cover key paths but not all eight combinations. Stretch: test all eight and justify coverage. Support: add intermediate (member or guest) results.","body":["Rule: a member or guest may enter if not suspended.","Write the condition with explicit parentheses.","Choose cases for each route to entry and refusal.","Predict, run and compare, then swap roles."],"minutes":12},{"title":"What does or allow?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Collect responses before discussion. Correct B: True or True is True. Explain inclusive or allows both. If learners choose A, use the truth-table first row and recheck True or False. Ask them to contrast and, which requires both.","body":["Predict True or True.","A: False     B: True","Show your choice, then explain it.","How would and differ when only one value is True?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a combined decision","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Rule: both age >= 16 and has_permission, reversed permission with not. Expected age 16/permission False gives False for eligibility, age 15/permission True gives False and age 16/permission True gives True. not False is True. Collect without Python first. Group follow-up support by comparison, conjunction or negation errors.","body":["Rule: age >= 16 and has_permission.","Predict for (16, False), (15, True), (16, True).","Predict not False.","Explain why one unsuccessful case fails."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Several choices with if and elif","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor and paper trace tables. Use the chapter’s supplied score-band rule.","slides":[{"title":"Several choices with if and elif","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: implement a three-way decision, trace the first matching branch and explain why order matters. This lesson uses the supplied score-band example from the chapter.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 3","Choose the first matching branch in an ordered decision."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: two-way selection","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"Ask learners to sketch if/else structure and mark indentation. Expected one of the two branches runs and a print after the decision runs either way. == compares while = assigns. Revisit structural errors before adding elif.","body":["Sketch an if/else decision from memory.","What determines which branch runs?","Where could you print a result after either branch?","Compare the indentation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A supplied score-band rule","strategy":"Requirement annotation","notes":"Assume a suitable whole-number score from 0 to 100. Model 85 as High. Ask learners independently for 65 Pass and 40 Developing. Have them underline the inclusive boundary at 80 and 50. These are example program categories, not official ATAR grade boundaries.","table":[["Score","Band"],["80 to 100","High"],["50 to 79","Pass"],["0 to 49","Developing"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"An ordered decision in Python","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided trace","notes":"For 80 the first comparison is True, so High runs and the remaining branches are skipped. For 65 the first is False, the elif is True and Pass runs. For 40 both comparisons are False and else runs. elif belongs to the same chain. Colon and indentation matter. Learners annotate the checked conditions, not only the output.","body":["Trace scores 80, 65 and 40."],"code":"score = 80\nif score >= 80:\n    band = \"High\"\nelif score >= 50:\n    band = \"Pass\"\nelse:\n    band = \"Developing\"\nprint(band)","minutes":10},{"title":"Why the order matters","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis","notes":"Take predictions before running. With 85, the first >= 50 comparison is True, so band becomes Pass and the elif is skipped. This is a logic error for the stated rule. Learners reorder the >= 80 check before >= 50 and test 85 again. If several still expect High automatically, trace line by line and cross out skipped branches.","body":["Which band does this code assign?","Explain the fault, then correct the branch order."],"code":"score = 85\nif score >= 50:\n    band = \"Pass\"\nelif score >= 80:\n    band = \"High\"\nelse:\n    band = \"Developing\"","minutes":10},{"title":"Your own implementation and test record","strategy":"Independent coding with optional scaffolds","notes":"Learners implement the correct ordered rule and record predictions before runs. Expected 49 Developing, 50 Pass, 79 Pass, 80 High. Support: provide a skeleton with blank conditions, then ask learners to explain their choices. Stretch: compare an ordered chain with two independent if statements and investigate whether later assignments overwrite a result. Keep the supplied 0–100 valid-input assumption.","body":["Implement the supplied score-band rule.","Predict bands for 49, 50, 79 and 80.","Run each case and retain your expected results.","Explain any correction using a trace."],"minutes":12},{"title":"One chain or separate decisions?","strategy":"Paired prediction and whole-class check","notes":"Trace this deliberate fault at score 85: first if assigns High; second independent if is also True and overwrites band with Pass. Output Pass. Contrast with the correct if/elif chain, which prints High. If learners expect High, ask which assignment runs last. This is about separate if statements, not a claim that all if statements should use elif.","body":["Predict the output before discussing it.","How does a second if differ from elif?"],"code":"score = 85\nif score >= 80:\n    band = \"High\"\nif score >= 50:\n    band = \"Pass\"\nprint(band)","minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: branch and reason","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected 79 Pass, 80 High and 50 Pass. 80 belongs to High because the >= 80 branch is checked first and includes equality. Use responses to select a short first-match tracing starter for Lesson 4.","body":["State the bands for scores 79, 80 and 50.","Which condition matches first for score 80?","Explain why checking >= 50 first causes a fault.","Save your code and test evidence."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w03-l04","lesson":4,"title":"Boundary tests and debugging","chapter":"selection","prepare":"Python editor, a clean delivery example and a blank expected/actual test log.","slides":[{"title":"Boundary tests and debugging","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success: derive tests from a requirement, identify a boundary fault, correct it and rerun the relevant tests. Expected results must come from the stated rule rather than the faulty code.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 3 · Lesson 4","Use expected results to find and explain a decision fault."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: where do results change?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Ask learners to identify the two score boundaries 50 and 80, then the delivery boundary 50. Rehearse below/at/above with valid integer scores: 49,50,51 and 79,80,81. For delivery totals to cents use 49.99,50.00,50.01. Distinguish the input domains rather than assuming every task uses the same step.","body":["Where do the score-band decisions change?","Where does the free-delivery decision change?","Choose a value just below one boundary.","Compare your reasoning with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A test record keeps expectation separate","strategy":"Worked example and guided completion","notes":"Delivery rule stays $0 for totals >= 50 and $5 otherwise. Model 49.99 with condition False and expected $5.00. Learners complete expected values for 50 and 50.01 before running. Actual and pass/fail remain blank until execution. Expected $0.00 for both. If learners copy actual into expected, revisit the requirement and preserve an independent prediction.","table":[["Input total","Expected charge","Actual / result"],["49.99","$5.00","Record after run"],["50.00","Predict from rule","Record after run"],["50.01","Predict from rule","Record after run"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"A fault that ordinary tests can miss","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Run this deliberately faulty example with 10 and 100: both match the stated rule. Then 50 prints Delivery: $5.00 but expectation is $0.00. The condition > excludes the boundary. Ask learners to record the mismatch before editing. Keep suitable non-negative input; invalid input handling is separate.","body":["Predict from the requirement for 10, 100 and 50.","Run each case and locate the mismatch."],"code":"order_total = float(input(\"Order total: \"))\nif order_total > 50:\n    delivery = 0\nelse:\n    delivery = 5\nprint(f\"Delivery: ${delivery:.2f}\")","minutes":10},{"title":"A correction needs an explanation","strategy":"Guided debugging and peer explanation","notes":"Learners explain that >= includes exactly 50, change the faulty comparison and rerun 49.99,50,50.01,10,100. Expected charges 5,0,0,5,0. Keep the earlier failed result alongside the corrected run. A successful test is evidence for tested cases, not proof for every possible input. Support: trace only the comparison at 50 first.","body":["Use a trace to explain the wrong charge at 50.","Correct the comparison and predict again.","Rerun below, at and above the boundary.","Retest ordinary cases and record the correction."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Transfer: museum ticket prices","strategy":"Independent design and testing","notes":"Rule age \u003c 16 costs $8, otherwise $12; valid non-negative integer ages. Learners choose 15,16,17 plus an ordinary younger/older case before coding. Model solution: age = int(input(\"Age: \")); if age \u003c 16: ticket = 8; else: ticket = 12; print(ticket), with proper indentation on separate lines. Expected 8,12,12. Support: supply an if/else skeleton. Stretch: show a \u003c= fault and identify the detecting input. Partners may question the test evidence after independent work.","body":["Under 16 costs $8. Everyone else pays $12.","Design boundary tests before writing the program.","Implement, run and record expected versus actual.","Ask a partner to challenge one test choice."],"minutes":12},{"title":"What do passing tests show?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Correct B: passing 10 and 100 does not establish that equality is handled. The > fault passes those two cases but fails 50. Collect responses before showing the faulty code again. If learners choose A, ask them to supply the missing boundary expectation. No finite small set proves all possible behaviours.","body":["Delivery tests at 10 and 100 both pass.","“This proves the boundary is correct.”","A: agree     B: disagree","Choose, then give a test that strengthens the evidence."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: justify a test set","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"For museum ages 15,16,17 expected 8,12,12. Age 16 detects \u003c= 16 used for the cheaper branch, because that fault gives 8 instead of 12. Learners explain how they derived expectations and one correction. Collect evidence to identify comparison, branch-order or test-design support before Week 4 loops.","body":["Give museum tests just below, at and above 16.","Write the expected prices from the rule.","Which test detects age \u003c= 16 as a fault?","Explain one check you will reuse in future programs."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w04-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Fixed repetition with for and range","chapter":"loops","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace grids and the loops chapter. Use whole-number donations for the example.","slides":[{"title":"Fixed repetition with for and range","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means identify what repeats, list range values and trace a fixed loop. Connect Week 3 selection, which chooses a path, with iteration, which repeats a body. Introduce the three-donation task from the chapter.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 4 · Lesson 1","Repeat a task a known number of times."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: sequence and selection","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"One minute individually, then compare explanations. Expected sequence executes instructions in order, selection chooses a branch, >= includes equality and total = total + 5 uses the old total to calculate a new total. Revisit assignment if learners interpret it as mathematical equality.","body":["What does an if/else decision choose?","Why does >= include a boundary value?","Explain total = total + 5 in words.","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"What repeats in the donation task?","strategy":"Teacher modelling and requirement annotation","notes":"Model reading and adding one donation, then ask what should repeat for three donations. Initialise total once before repetition and display once afterwards. The loop body reads and adds. Assume suitable non-negative whole-number amounts. Use three counters or slips to act out passes before code.","body":["The club records three whole-number donations.","Read one amount and add it to a running total.","Repeat those steps for each donation.","Display the final total after all three."],"minutes":6},{"title":"range leaves out the stop value","strategy":"Worked examples and guided prediction","notes":"Model range(3) as 0,1,2 and range(1,4) as 1,2,3. Both produce three values. Ask everyone to predict range(2,6): 2,3,4,5, then check. In these positive-step examples stop is excluded. range(0) produces no values. Python for iterates over these values and assigns each to the loop variable.","table":[["Expression","Values","Passes"],["range(3)","0, 1, 2","3"],["range(1, 4)","1, 2, 3","3"],["range(2, 6)","Predict first","Count the values"],["range(0)","No values","0"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A running total across three passes","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and code annotation","notes":"Think aloud through 5,7,3: total starts 0, then becomes 5,12,15. donation_number labels passes 1,2,3. Highlight the two indented lines as the body. final print is outside the loop and displays Total: $15 once. Ask learners to point to initialisation and the repeated update. The range ends repetition after the third value.","body":["Trace donations 5, 7 and 3.","Which lines repeat and which run once?"],"code":"total = 0\nfor donation_number in range(1, 4):\n    donation = int(input(\"Donation: \"))\n    total = total + donation\nprint(f\"Total: ${total}\")","minutes":10},{"title":"Prediction before paired runs","strategy":"Paired programming with role changes","notes":"Driver runs the example while navigator checks the prior prediction and explains a pass, then swap roles for a second set. First set 5,7,3 gives 15; second 0,4,6 gives 10. Every learner records totals after each pass. Support: provide total-before and total-after columns. Stretch: change to four donations, predict a new input set and explain the changed range stop.","body":["Write the total after each input: 5, 7, 3.","Run the program and compare with your trace.","Swap roles and predict for 0, 4, 6.","Explain how the loop knows when to finish."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which range gives four numbered passes?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B: range(1,5) produces 1,2,3,4. A produces three values and C produces five values 0 through 4. Collect simultaneous responses before discussion. If several choose A, list values explicitly and recheck range(1,3). Distinguish number of passes from the final stop argument.","body":["The passes must be numbered 1, 2, 3, 4.","A: range(1, 4)     B: range(1, 5)     C: range(5)","Choose alone, then list the values.","Explain why the two other choices do not fit."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: explain a fixed loop","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Expected range(2,5) gives 2,3,4 and three passes. With donations 2,4,6, running totals are 2,6,12. Initialisation stays before the loop and final output after it. Collect individual traces to identify range-stop or accumulation support for Lesson 2.","body":["List the values in range(2, 5).","Trace totals for donations 2, 4 and 6.","Where should total start at zero?","Explain why the final print sits outside the loop."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w04-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Condition-controlled while loops","chapter":"loops","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace grids and a known school-editor Stop command. Discuss non-termination on paper before running.","slides":[{"title":"Condition-controlled while loops","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means trace a pre-test while loop, identify initialisation and update, and explain how the condition becomes False. Begin with a small bounded counter so learners can compare with for.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 4 · Lesson 2","Repeat while a condition remains True."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the range boundary","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired explanation","notes":"Expected range(1,4) produces 1,2,3 and excludes 4. A final print outside the loop runs once. Initialising a total inside the loop discards earlier accumulated values. Ask learners to retrieve before notes, then correct their explanation.","body":["List the values in range(1, 4).","Why does the value 4 stay out?","Which instruction normally prints the final total once?","Compare your answers with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A condition checked before every pass","strategy":"Teacher modelling and guided prediction","notes":"Trace count=1, condition True, print 1, update to 2. Continue through 3 then update to 4. At 4 the condition is False, so the body stops and Finished prints. Output 1,2,3,Finished on separate lines. Check the condition before entry and after each update. Ask a learner to narrate the last failed check.","body":["Predict the output before running.","What value makes the condition False?"],"code":"count = 1\nwhile count \u003c= 3:\n    print(count)\n    count = count + 1\nprint(\"Finished\")","minutes":6},{"title":"The final check belongs in the trace","strategy":"Worked trace with faded support","notes":"Model the first two rows, then learners complete rows for count 3 and 4. At 3, True prints 3 and updates to 4. At 4, False prints nothing inside the body and performs no update. Support: keep condition column headings. Stretch: explain why there are four condition checks but only three body executions.","table":[["Count at check","count \u003c= 3","Body and update"],["1","True","Print 1, count becomes 2"],["2","True","Print 2, count becomes 3"],["3","Predict first","Complete the trace"],["4","Predict first","Does the body run?"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A while loop may run zero times","strategy":"Guided variation and peer explanation","notes":"Change the start value to 4. The first condition is False, so only Finished prints. For starts 3 and 1, outputs are 3 then Finished, and 1,2,3 then Finished. Ask learners to write predictions before editing. A pre-test loop can have zero body executions; reaching the while line does not guarantee a pass.","body":["Keep while count \u003c= 3.","Predict when count starts at 4, then at 3.","Run each version and compare.","Explain why reaching a loop can produce no passes."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Zero, one and several passes","strategy":"Paired investigation and role changes","notes":"Pairs run the same bounded loop with starts 4,3,1 after individual predictions. Driver enters the starting value in source, navigator checks the condition trace, then swap roles. Every learner records number of passes: 0,1,3. Support: circle the start and update. Stretch: choose a new upper limit and design zero/one/several-pass tests without adding input validation.","table":[["Starting count","Expected passes","Actual passes"],["4","Predict first","Record after run"],["3","Predict first","Record after run"],["1","Predict first","Record after run"]],"minutes":12},{"title":"What would stop this repetition?","strategy":"Paper debugging and all-student check","notes":"Discuss without executing an unbounded loop. If count starts at 1 and while count \u003c= 3 has no update, printing count never changes the condition. It remains True. Expected repair: count = count + 1 inside the body. Model the school editor Stop control before learners experiment, then run only the corrected bounded version. Recheck termination by tracing count=4.","body":["On paper: count starts at 1.","The body prints count but never changes it.","Will while count \u003c= 3 ever become False?","Write an update that makes this example finish."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: the stopping check","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For count=1, while count \u003c 3 prints and increments, output is 1 and 2. Last update makes count 3, then condition False. Start at 3 gives zero passes. Ask for the final failed condition check rather than only the printed sequence. Use errors to revisit \u003c versus \u003c= next lesson.","body":["Start count at 1 and use while count \u003c 3.","Print count, then add 1 on each pass.","State the output and the value at the stopping check.","What happens if count starts at 3?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w04-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Counters, totals and loop faults","chapter":"loops","prepare":"Python editor, paper trace grids and a clean three-donation program.","slides":[{"title":"Counters, totals and loop faults","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means distinguish counter from accumulator, trace both and explain a repair. Use the same donation example to reduce new context while learners reason about changing state.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 4 · Lesson 3","Track what changes and explain why a loop gives its result."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the loop structure","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Expected initialise before repetition, update changing state inside the body and trace the failed condition for while. A fixed known number of passes often suits for/range. Ask learners to explain how an update changes termination, then compare with notes.","body":["Name something initialised before a loop.","What update can make a while condition become False?","When does a known pass count suit for/range?","Compare one explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Counting passes and accumulating values","strategy":"Worked example and code annotation","notes":"Model inputs 5,7,3. count changes 0,1,2,3 while total changes 0,5,12,15. count increases by 1 per donation and total increases by the entered amount. final print displays 3 15. The name pass_number receives the values 0, 1 and 2; the separate count records completed inputs. Ask learners to mark both updates.","body":["Which variable counts passes?","Which variable combines the input amounts?"],"code":"count = 0\ntotal = 0\nfor pass_number in range(3):\n    donation = int(input(\"Donation: \"))\n    count = count + 1\n    total = total + donation\nprint(count, total)","minutes":6},{"title":"A trace of two changing values","strategy":"Guided practice with reduced support","notes":"Learners complete independently: after 5 count1 total5; after 7 count2 total12; after 3 count3 total15. Ask them to compare the update rules in pairs. Support: model only the first row and provide columns. Stretch: trace a zero donation and explain why count changes even when total does not.","table":[["Donation","Count after pass","Total after pass"],["5","Complete the trace","Complete the trace"],["7","Complete the trace","Complete the trace"],["3","Complete the trace","Complete the trace"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A total that forgets earlier values","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"This deliberate fault resets total to zero during every pass. With 5,7,3, final total is 3. Learners predict from the intended requirement as 15, then trace the code’s actual 3, preserving the distinction. Repair by moving total = 0 before the loop. Do not change the expected result to match the faulty program.","body":["Predict the final value for 5, 7 and 3.","Explain where earlier donations disappear."],"code":"for pass_number in range(3):\n    total = 0\n    donation = int(input(\"Donation: \"))\n    total = total + donation\nprint(total)","minutes":10},{"title":"A repair supported by tests","strategy":"Independent debugging and peer feedback","notes":"Learners repair reset placement and run input sets 5,7,3 ->15; 0,4,6 ->10; 0,0,0 ->0. Keep before/after traces. Ask why only testing zeros would hide the fault: both correct and faulty versions give 0. Support: show the indentation boundary. Stretch: add a counter and verify 3 even for all-zero inputs. Peer checks should challenge evidence without taking over code.","body":["Move the initialisation to the correct place.","Predict and test 5,7,3 and 0,4,6.","Also test three zeros.","Explain why one passing input set is weak evidence."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which change affects which value?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"If donation=0, count still increases by 1 and total stays the same. Correct B. Collect simultaneous responses, then ask learners to point to the two update expressions. If several choose A, trace an initial count0,total0 with one zero donation. Counters track events and accumulators combine values, so they need not be equal.","body":["A donation is 0. What happens after that pass?","A: neither count nor total changes","B: count increases and total stays the same","Choose alone and justify from the update rules."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: a trace and a repair","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For donations 2,0,4, final count3,total6. total starts at 0 before the loop, then becomes 2,2,6. Ask learners to explain why zero input still counts as a donation. A reset inside the loop would produce final total4. Use responses to decide whether to provide the score-task skeleton next lesson.","body":["Trace donations 2, 0 and 4.","State final count and total.","Explain why the zero donation still counts as a pass.","Where must total start at zero, and why?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w04-l04","lesson":4,"title":"A score total and mean","chapter":"loops","prepare":"Python editor and a blank expected/actual log. Assume four suitable whole-number scores.","slides":[{"title":"A score total and mean","strategy":"Learning intention and transfer","notes":"Success means plan and implement four inputs, accumulate their sum, calculate mean after repetition and justify tests. This is formative transfer practice from the chapter, not an extra summative assessment.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 4 · Lesson 4","Build and test a program that reads four scores."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: what repeats and what runs once?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair explanation","notes":"Expected input and total update repeat, total initialisation and final mean/output run once. For four passes range(4) gives 0,1,2,3. Allow individual answers before pairs discuss. Revisit accumulator placement if students put total=0 in the repeated body.","body":["Which donation instructions repeated each pass?","Which instructions ran once?","List the values produced by range(4).","Explain the accumulator’s starting value."],"minutes":5},{"title":"The score task requirements","strategy":"Requirement annotation and guided planning","notes":"Exactly four whole-number scores, suitable input assumed. The program must display sum and arithmetic mean. Learners underline four, total and mean. Use for/range because pass count is known. Choose a non-negative integer score assumption for all class tests and keep invalid text outside the core task.","body":["Read exactly four whole-number scores.","Display their total and arithmetic mean.","Assume suitable non-negative scores.","Choose a loop and explain why it fits."],"minutes":6},{"title":"Expected results before implementation","strategy":"Independent prediction and pair comparison","notes":"Calculate independently before showing answers: 8+10+6+12=36, mean36/4=9.0. For 0,0,0,0 expect total0 mean0.0. For 5,5,5,5 expect20 and5.0. Ask learners for working, not Python output. Support: add the first two scores together before the next two. Stretch: choose inputs with a non-integer mean, such as 1,2,3,5 ->11 and2.75.","table":[["Four scores","Expected total","Expected mean"],["8, 10, 6, 12","Calculate by hand","Total divided by 4"],["0, 0, 0, 0","Calculate by hand","Total divided by 4"],["5, 5, 5, 5","Calculate by hand","Total divided by 4"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A plan with a clear loop body","strategy":"Teacher modelling with faded support","notes":"Model the algorithm verbally, leaving Python implementation to students. Initialise total once, repeat read/add four times, then divide by four and display both results. In Python range(4) repeats four times. Explain that += is shorthand for assigning old total plus score, if learners use the chapter’s solution. Support: offer a blank loop skeleton. Stretch: explain why / preserves a fractional mean while // would discard its fraction for these non-negative inputs.","body":["Set the running total to zero.","Repeat: read one score and add it, four times.","After the loop, calculate total / 4.","Display the total and mean."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Your implementation and test record","strategy":"Independent coding with optional scaffolds","notes":"Reference solution: total=0; for score_number in range(4): read score with int(input(\"Score: \")) then total=total+score; after the loop mean=total/4; print(total,mean). Use proper newlines and indentation. Learners run all three predicted sets, retaining expectations if actual differs. Support: provide the input line and blank loop boundaries. Stretch: use 1,2,3,5 to test a fractional mean. Ask each learner to explain a chosen line without partner help.","body":["Write the program from your plan.","Run each predicted input set and record actual results.","Trace the first wrong value if a result differs.","Save your code and evidence."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Three scores or four?","strategy":"Hinge question and debugging explanation","notes":"A loop using range(3) reads only three scores. With 8,10,6, it accumulates24, then dividing by4 gives6.0. The intended four-score result is36 and9.0. Correct the range to four passes; dividing by3 alone would still violate the four-input requirement. Collect simultaneous responses before discussion and ask learners to cite the requirement.","body":["A version reads scores with range(3).","It then calculates mean = total / 4.","What requirement does it miss?","Which change fixes the input count?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: defend your solution","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"For 1,2,3,5, expected total11 and mean2.75. Learners identify exactly four passes and why mean runs afterwards. Ask what their tests can and cannot establish. Check understanding of counting, accumulation and integer versus real division before Week 5 functions. Save individual work for formative follow-up.","body":["Predict total and mean for 1, 2, 3 and 5.","Explain how your program reads exactly four scores.","Why calculate the final mean after the loop?","Name a test that helped you find or avoid a fault."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w05-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Function definitions and calls","chapter":"functions","prepare":"Python editor, the functions chapter and the familiar badge-cost calculation.","slides":[{"title":"Function definitions and calls","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means identify a definition, make a call and trace a returned result. Link to earlier badge programs so learners focus on function structure rather than a new calculation.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 5 · Lesson 1","Give a useful calculation a name and call it when needed."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: a familiar calculation","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Allow one minute without notes, then compare. Four badges at $3 cost $12. Quantity and price are inputs to the calculation; multiplication produces the result. Distinguish calculation from input prompts and printing. Revisit arithmetic if needed before introducing syntax.","body":["Four badges cost $3 each. What is the total?","Which values does the calculation need?","Which step calculates, and which step displays?","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A function’s agreed task","strategy":"Requirement annotation and teacher modelling","notes":"Explain contract as agreed inputs, result and assumptions. Use non-negative whole-number quantities and whole-dollar prices in these introductory examples. The function returns quantity × unit_price. It does not prompt or print. The caller decides where values come from and how to use the result.","table":[["Part of the contract","Badge example"],["Name","badge_cost"],["Inputs","quantity and unit_price"],["Returned result","quantity × unit_price"],["Responsibility","Calculate the cost"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"A definition waits for a call","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided prediction","notes":"def creates the function, but its body runs when badge_cost(4,3) is called. Parameters receive 4 and 3, return sends 12 back, cost receives 12 and print displays it. Mark the indented body and unindented caller. Ask why a file containing only this definition prints nothing.","body":["Predict what prints and when the body runs.","Identify the definition and the call."],"code":"def badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):\n    return quantity * unit_price\n\ncost = badge_cost(4, 3)\nprint(cost)","minutes":10},{"title":"A trace across the call","strategy":"Worked example and faded support","notes":"Model 4,3 as arguments, local parameter bindings and returned 12 stored in cost. Learners independently trace badge_cost(6,3): result 18. Support: provide stage headings. Stretch: trace badge_cost(0,3) and explain why it needs no special branch.","table":[["Stage","For badge_cost(4, 3)"],["Caller supplies arguments","4 and 3"],["Parameters receive values","quantity = 4, unit_price = 3"],["Function returns","12"],["Caller stores result","cost = 12"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Several calls, one definition","strategy":"Paired prediction and coding","notes":"Keep one definition and make separate calls (0,3), (4,3), (4,5). Expected 0,12,20. Driver runs, navigator checks prior predictions and explains a call, then swap. Each learner records results. Support: supply one example call. Stretch: use a returned result in a later addition without changing the function.","body":["Keep one badge_cost definition.","Predict calls with (0, 3), (4, 3) and (4, 5).","Run each call and record the returned result.","Swap roles and explain how the same body serves each call."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which line calls the function?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B invokes the function. A defines it and C refers to the function object without calling it. Take simultaneous choices. If several choose A, run a file containing only the definition and ask what triggers its body.","body":["A: def badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):","B: badge_cost(4, 3)","C: badge_cost","Choose the call and explain what triggers execution."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: define, call and predict","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"badge_cost(5,2) returns 10, stored with cost = badge_cost(5,2). Definition creates the function; a call invokes it. Collect individual calls and explanations. Use errors to group definition-versus-call support next lesson.","body":["Write a call for five badges at $2 each.","Predict the returned result.","Show how the caller could store that result.","Explain how a definition differs from a call."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w05-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Parameters, arguments and returns","chapter":"functions","prepare":"Python editor, badge_cost definition and a paper call-trace template.","slides":[{"title":"Parameters, arguments and returns","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means map arguments to parameters, use a return value and distinguish returned results from displayed output.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 5 · Lesson 2","Trace values into a function and back to its caller."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the first call","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"Expected badge_cost(4,3) returns 12. The body runs on a call. A caller can assign its returned result to cost. Allow individual retrieval then pair comparison. Retrace the call if learners think def immediately computes a numeric result.","body":["Predict badge_cost(4, 3).","When does the indented function body run?","How can the caller keep the returned result?","Compare one answer with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Parameters and arguments","strategy":"Worked mapping and guided practice","notes":"Parameters width,height appear in def area(width,height). Arguments 5,2 appear in area(5,2). Positional binding maps first to first, second to second. Product 10 returns. Learners then map badge_cost(6,4): quantity 6, price 4, result 24. Support: align columns. Keyword and default arguments come later.","table":[["Definition or call","What to identify"],["def area(width, height):","Parameters: width and height"],["area(5, 2)","Arguments: 5 and 2"],["Inside this call","width = 5, height = 2"],["Returned product","10"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"The caller can use the result","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided prediction","notes":"cost is 12, payable 14 and output 14. Trace entry into the function, return to assignment, then caller addition. Packaging is a separate caller calculation. Ask learners to predict badge_cost(2,5) plus 2:12. A result can be stored, displayed or combined.","body":["Track cost, then payable.","Which calculation happens in the caller?"],"code":"def badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):\n    return quantity * unit_price\n\ncost = badge_cost(4, 3)\npayable = cost + 2\nprint(payable)","minutes":10},{"title":"Displaying and returning differ","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"This deliberately faulty calculation prints 12 inside the function, then caller prints None. Normal completion without an explicit return value returns None. Learners predict both lines, run and explain. Replace inner print with return quantity * unit_price. Corrected caller prints 12 once. A later numeric calculation needs a numeric returned result.","body":["Predict both lines of output.","What value does the caller store in cost?"],"code":"def badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):\n    print(quantity * unit_price)\n\ncost = badge_cost(4, 3)\nprint(cost)","minutes":10},{"title":"A result the caller can reuse","strategy":"Paired debugging with role changes","notes":"Repair print to return. Test (0,3)->0, (4,3)->12, (4,5)->20. The last result plus caller packaging 2 gives 22. Driver repairs while navigator traces the return, then swap. Input and printing stay outside. Support: annotate return-to-assignment. Stretch: explain how an extra print inside would add output without changing a numeric return.","body":["Repair the function so it returns the product.","Test calls with (0, 3), (4, 3) and (4, 5).","Use the last result in a caller calculation that adds 2.","Swap roles and explain the path back to the caller."],"minutes":12},{"title":"What does the call return?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"Correct B: normal completion with no explicit return value gives None. Displaying 12 does not return 12. Take responses before discussion. If several choose A, assign a call result and inspect it separately. return ends that call; print displays output.","body":["A function prints 12 and reaches the end.","It has no explicit return statement.","What does the call return? A: 12     B: None","Choose alone and explain the difference."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: map and use the values","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"Parameters width,height receive arguments 7,2 and product 14 returns. Adding 3 in caller gives 17. Collect a labelled mapping and result. Revisit vocabulary or return/None according to errors.","body":["For def area(width, height) and area(7, 2):","Name the parameters and arguments.","Predict the returned product.","If the caller adds 3, what is its new result?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w05-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Local names and program structure","chapter":"functions","prepare":"Python editor and separate caller/function trace columns.","slides":[{"title":"Local names and program structure","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means distinguish local and module-level names, trace simple shadowing and refactor a calculation with explicit inputs and result. Global means module-level in these examples.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 5 · Lesson 3","Keep a function’s local work separate from its caller."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: a reusable result","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired explanation","notes":"Parameters appear in definition, arguments in call, return sends a value to caller, print displays output. Retrieve individually then compare before a brief correction. This prepares scope without introducing a new calculation.","body":["What are parameters and arguments?","Why return a result that another calculation needs?","What does a call without an explicit return value return?","Compare and correct one explanation."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Where names belong","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided trace","notes":"Output 12 then 2. Global quantity starts 2. Local parameter quantity receives 4 and unit_price 3; local result 12 returns and caller cost becomes 12. This does not rebind global quantity. Ask learners to annotate caller and local columns. Same spelling does not imply the same binding.","body":["Predict both printed values.","Which quantity belongs to the current call?"],"code":"quantity = 2\ndef badge_cost(quantity, unit_price):\n    result = quantity * unit_price\n    return result\ncost = badge_cost(4, 3)\nprint(cost)\nprint(quantity)","minutes":6},{"title":"A scope record for one call","strategy":"Worked example with reduced support","notes":"Model global quantity 2 and local quantity 4, then learners complete local result 12 and caller cost 12. A fresh call badge_cost(6,5) returns 30 while global quantity stays 2. Parameters and assigned names inside this function are local. Module-level code cannot directly use its local result name.","table":[["Name and location","Value for badge_cost(4, 3)"],["quantity at module level","2"],["quantity inside badge_cost","4"],["unit_price inside badge_cost","3"],["result inside badge_cost","Complete the trace"],["cost after return to caller","Complete the trace"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"A local assignment","strategy":"Predict, run, investigate","notes":"Output 9 then 2. x=9 creates a local binding; global x remains 2. The caller receives 9 through return. A function can read global names, but local assignment does not automatically change a global. Global declarations are outside the core task. Avoid saying globals cannot be read inside functions.","body":["Predict before running.","Explain why the two outputs differ."],"code":"x = 2\ndef local_demo():\n    x = 9\n    return x\nprint(local_demo())\nprint(x)","minutes":10},{"title":"Refactoring the badge calculation","strategy":"Independent refactoring and peer explanation","notes":"Write badge_cost(quantity,unit_price) returning product. Keep prompts and receipt output in caller. Test (0,3)->0, (4,3)->12, (4,5)->20 before interactive input. Explicit parameters make dependencies visible rather than relying on changing global prices. Support: provide a definition skeleton. Stretch: explain why a new price argument changes that call’s result. Peer checks compare behaviour before/after.","body":["Move the badge calculation into badge_cost.","Pass quantity and price as arguments.","Keep input prompts and receipt printing in the caller.","Test the calculation first, then the whole program."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Does the global value change?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct A: module-level x stays 2 in local_demo. B is the returned local value 9. Take responses before discussion. If several choose B, use separate trace columns. Returning 9 differs from assigning a new global x.","body":["In local_demo, a local x is set to 9.","The module-level x started at 2.","After the call, module-level x is A: 2     B: 9","Choose alone and justify using scope."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: trace scope","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"With global quantity 2 and badge_cost(3,5), result 15 returns, local quantity 3 and global quantity 2. Caller receives the local calculation through return. Collect individual traces and use errors to plan support for the delivery function.","body":["Keep module-level quantity at 2.","Call badge_cost(3, 5) and predict its result.","State the local and module-level quantity values.","How can the caller receive a locally calculated result?"],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w05-l04","lesson":4,"title":"A delivery function with tests","chapter":"functions","prepare":"Python editor and input/expected/actual log. Assume suitable non-negative numeric order totals.","slides":[{"title":"A delivery function with tests","strategy":"Learning intention and transfer","notes":"Success means implement a contract, return the correct charge and test below/at/above 50. Formative transfer uses the chapter’s existing delivery rule.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 5 · Lesson 4","Test a focused function before adding input and output."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: the delivery boundary","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair comparison","notes":"Totals 49.99,50,50.01 give charges 5,0,0. >=50 includes equality. return supplies a result, print displays output. Retrieve the Week 3 rule individually then compare before designing the function.","body":["Recall free delivery for orders of $50 or more.","Predict charges for 49.99, 50 and 50.01.","Which comparison includes exactly 50?","How does return differ from print?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"A small function contract","strategy":"Requirement annotation","notes":"Name delivery_charge, one parameter order_total, numeric returned charge. Assume suitable non-negative finite numbers. Invalid text/negative totals are outside this introductory contract. Input and print stay outside the function. Learners underline >=50 and identify caller operations.","table":[["Part","Requirement"],["Name","delivery_charge"],["Parameter","order_total"],["Return for total >= 50","0"],["Return for smaller suitable totals","5"],["Input prompts and output","Stay in the caller"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"An early return ends the call","strategy":"Teacher modelling and guided prediction","notes":"For 50, True returns 0 and ends the call, so return 5 does not run. For 49.99, False leads to return 5. print with 50 displays 0. Learners predict 50.01->0 before running. An explicit else returning 5 is also valid. Focus on returned charge rather than formatted receipt text.","body":["Trace totals 50 and 49.99.","Why does a successful call return only one charge?"],"code":"def delivery_charge(order_total):\n    if order_total >= 50:\n        return 0\n    return 5\n\nprint(delivery_charge(50))","minutes":10},{"title":"Expectations come from the contract","strategy":"Independent prediction with guided review","notes":"Before running, calculate 49.99->5,50->0,50.01->0. These distinguish > from >=. Add ordinary 10->5 and 100->0. Support: mark the inclusive boundary on a number line. Stretch: explain why 10 and 100 alone miss equality faults.","table":[["Order total","Expected charge","Actual / result"],["49.99","Predict from rule","Record after run"],["50.00","Predict from rule","Record after run"],["50.01","Predict from rule","Record after run"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Your implementation and caller","strategy":"Independent coding with optional scaffolds","notes":"Re-create from the contract before consulting the model. Make separate calls for boundary totals and 10,100, recording expected/actual. Then add total = float(input(\"Order total: \")), charge = delivery_charge(total), and print(charge) in caller. Support: header and blank branches. Stretch: store and reuse returned charge in another calculation. No input validation is claimed.","body":["Write delivery_charge from the contract.","Test boundary totals and ordinary totals 10 and 100.","Then add input and display in the caller.","Record any correction and rerun the test set."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Which test detects the equality fault?","strategy":"Hinge question and debugging explanation","notes":"A faulty >50 condition returns 5 at 50 instead of 0. Correct B. 49.99 and 100 still pass. Take simultaneous responses and explain why the repair needs rerunning other tests. If several choose A, compare > and >= at exactly 50 in a trace.","body":["A faulty function uses order_total > 50.","Which input reveals the error?","A: 49.99     B: 50     C: 100","Choose alone, explain and propose the correction."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: defend the function","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"For 50 charge 0; for 25 charge 5. Returning a number supports reuse. Strong evidence includes independent predictions below/at/above 50 with observed results. Collect individual code/test explanations to check calls, parameter mapping, return and scope before Week 6 arrays.","body":["State the returned charges for totals 50 and 25.","Explain why input stays outside the function.","Name the test that checks equality at the boundary.","Show how the caller can store and display a result."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w06-l01","lesson":1,"title":"Lists, indices and traversal","chapter":"arrays","prepare":"Python editor, paper index/value grids and the arrays chapter. Use suitable integer input.","slides":[{"title":"Lists, indices and traversal","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means load a list, identify a value by index and display each index/value pair. Explain that Python lists implement these array-style tasks. Lists can change size and hold mixed types, while some arrays have a fixed size/type. Use consistent integer values in this week’s exercises.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 6 · Lesson 1","Store related values and visit them by position."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: repeated input","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair explanation","notes":"Allow a minute without notes, then compare. range(3) gives 0,1,2 and three passes. A running total combines inputs but does not retain each original value. Ask why storing all readings might help later. This leads to a list without suggesting lists and fixed-size arrays are identical.","body":["List the values in range(3).","What does a running total retain?","How could keeping every original input help?","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A position differs from its value","strategy":"Worked example and guided prediction","notes":"Model scores=[12,7,19]. The first non-negative index is 0, scores[1] is 7 and len(scores) is 3. Ask for index of value 19:2. The last valid non-negative index is len(scores)-1. Python also supports negative indexing, which is outside this introductory convention. One-dimensional access uses one index.","table":[["Index","0","1","2"],["Value","12","7","19"]],"minutes":6},{"title":"Loading values with append","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and code annotation","notes":"Trace suitable integer inputs 12,7,19. The empty list grows to [12], then [12,7], then [12,7,19]. append adds at the end. Initialising scores=[] happens once before repetition. int converts input text. Ask learners to mark repeated instructions and predict len(scores) after each pass:1,2,3.","body":["Trace inputs 12, 7 and 19.","Which instruction retains each new value?"],"code":"scores = []\nfor index in range(3):\n    score = int(input(\"Score: \"))\n    scores.append(score)\nprint(scores)","minutes":10},{"title":"Traversal with each index","strategy":"Worked example and faded support","notes":"Expected output pairs 0 12,1 7,2 19. range(len(scores)) produces 0,1,2, so every stored element is visited once. Learners identify which expression is an index and which accesses a value. Support: provide a three-column trace. Stretch: explain why range(len(scores)+1) would attempt an invalid final index.","body":["Predict each index/value pair.","Explain why len(scores) is the stop value."],"code":"scores = [12, 7, 19]\nfor index in range(len(scores)):\n    print(index, scores[index])","minutes":10},{"title":"Your loading and display program","strategy":"Paired programming with role changes","notes":"Pairs load four integers then print each index/value pair. Use 8,3,6,10: indices 0..3 and matching values. Driver types loading, navigator checks a trace, then swap for display. Every learner records predictions. Support: supply an empty list and loop headers. Stretch: replace a source value and explain why its index stays the same. Keep suitable input assumption explicit.","body":["Load four integers using append.","Display every index and its stored value.","Predict first for inputs 8, 3, 6 and 10.","Swap roles and check that all four values appear once."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Where is the last valid index?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"For [12,7,19], correct B is 2. A is last value 19, C is length 3. scores[3] raises IndexError. Collect simultaneous choices before discussion. If several choose C, label positions on paper and recheck a two-element list. Do not imply all out-of-range Python indices are positive.","body":["scores = [12, 7, 19]","Its last valid non-negative index is:","A: 19     B: 2     C: 3","Choose alone and explain index versus value."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: access and explain","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For values=[8,3,6], values[1]=3, length 3 and last valid non-negative index 2. Access uses square brackets; append adds one value. Collect individual answers to identify indexing or length support for accumulator traversal next lesson.","body":["For values = [8, 3, 6], find values[1].","State the length and last non-negative index.","Explain what append does when loading input.","Describe how a traversal visits every value."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w06-l02","lesson":2,"title":"Totals with list traversal","chapter":"arrays","prepare":"Python editor, paper accumulator traces and suitable integer lists.","slides":[{"title":"Totals with list traversal","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means initialise an accumulator, add every list value once and test empty, negative and ordinary cases. Link to Week 4 running totals, now processing retained values.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 6 · Lesson 2","Accumulate a total by visiting every stored value."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: index or value?","strategy":"Individual retrieval and paired correction","notes":"For values=[4,-2,6], values[1]=-2, len 3 and non-negative indices 0,1,2. Ask learners to retrieve individually then compare. Revisit position versus value before a loop adds values rather than indices.","body":["values = [4, -2, 6]","Find values[1] and len(values).","List its non-negative indices.","Compare your answers with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"A running total through the values","strategy":"Teacher think-aloud and guided prediction","notes":"Trace total 0, then 4, then 2, then 8. for value in values supplies each stored value, not each index. += in the chapter is shorthand for total=total+value; use expanded form here. Final print outside loop runs once. Ask learners to explain why total starts before traversal.","body":["Predict total after each pass.","Which name receives a stored value?"],"code":"values = [4, -2, 6]\ntotal = 0\nfor value in values:\n    total = total + value\nprint(total)","minutes":6},{"title":"The accumulator trace","strategy":"Guided practice with faded support","notes":"Model first row 0+4=4, learners complete total before/after:4 and 2 for -2, then 2 and 8 for 6. Pair explanations should identify negative addition reducing the running total. Support: give one completed row. Stretch: write an indexed traversal yielding the same total and explain why adding index would give a different result.","table":[["Value","Total before","Total after"],["4","0","4"],["-2","Complete","Complete"],["6","Complete","Complete"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"An empty traversal still has a total","strategy":"Guided variation and peer explanation","notes":"With values=[], body runs zero times and initial total 0 prints. With[-8,-3], total becomes-8 then-11. With[5], one pass gives 5. Learners predict before running. This total algorithm safely handles an empty list because it does not read values[0]. Distinguish this from finding an empty list’s maximum next lesson.","body":["Keep the same total algorithm.","Predict for [], [-8, -3] and [5].","Run each case and compare.","Explain why an empty list needs no index access here."],"minutes":10},{"title":"Repairing a reset inside the loop","strategy":"Paired debugging and role changes","notes":"This deliberately faulty code outputs 6 rather than intended 8 for[4,-2,6]. Each pass resets total, discarding previous values. Move total=0 before the loop. Swap roles and test[],[5],[-8,-3],[4,-2,6], expecting 0,5,-11,8. Faulty version with empty list would have no total binding in a fresh run; repair before testing it. Support: mark indentation. Stretch: explain why[0,0] alone hides the reset fault.","body":["Predict this version’s output.","Repair it and test empty, single and negative cases."],"code":"values = [4, -2, 6]\nfor value in values:\n    total = 0\n    total = total + value\nprint(total)","minutes":12},{"title":"Does the loop add values or indices?","strategy":"All-student misconception check","notes":"For values=[10,20,30], summing indices 0+1+2 gives 3. Correct B. The required value total is 60. Collect simultaneous responses; if several choose A, place index and value columns side by side. range(len(values)) supplies indices, so an indexed accumulator must add values[index].","body":["values = [10, 20, 30]","A loop adds index for index in range(len(values)).","Its total is A: 60     B: 3","Choose and explain how to total the stored values."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: totals and evidence","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For[3,-1,2], running totals 3,2,4. Empty list total 0. Initialising before loop preserves earlier values. An unequal nonzero list exposes a reset error better than all zeros. Collect a trace and one justified test; use errors for a short accumulator refresher next lesson.","body":["Trace the total for [3, -1, 2].","State the expected total for an empty list.","Explain where initialisation belongs.","Choose a test that exposes a reset inside the loop."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w06-l03","lesson":3,"title":"Minimum and maximum positions","chapter":"arrays","prepare":"Python editor and index/value/best-index trace grids. Establish non-empty input before inspecting index0.","slides":[{"title":"Minimum and maximum positions","strategy":"Learning intention","notes":"Success means retain the position of a best value, explain first-occurrence ties and state empty-list assumptions. Use non-empty integer lists for the main algorithm, then explicitly discuss the empty case.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 6 · Lesson 3","Keep a best value’s index while traversing a list."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: location and value","strategy":"Individual retrieval and pair explanation","notes":"For values=[4,9,9], maximum value 9 appears at indices 1 and 2. Ask learners for both, then why saying 9 does not locate an occurrence. Retrieve zero-based indexing and length before deciding a tie rule.","body":["values = [4, 9, 9]","What is the maximum value?","At which indices does it occur?","Explain why a value alone does not identify its position."],"minutes":5},{"title":"The first maximum index","strategy":"Teacher modelling and guided trace","notes":"This example assumes non-empty values. Start best_index 0, comparing actual values via indexing. At index 1,9>4 updates best_index 1; at index 2,9>9 is False so retain 1. Output 1 9. State first-occurrence tie rule. best_index 0 means a valid position, not a guessed maximum value of zero.","body":["Assume the list is non-empty.","Trace which index the algorithm retains."],"code":"values = [4, 9, 9]\nbest_index = 0\nfor index in range(1, len(values)):\n    if values[index] > values[best_index]:\n        best_index = index\nprint(best_index, values[best_index])","minutes":6},{"title":"A trace of the best position","strategy":"Worked example with reduced support","notes":"Model initial best_index 0/value 4. For index 1 compare 9>4 True and update 1; index 2 compare 9>9 False and retain 1. Learners complete rows independently before pair checking. For a fresh[8,2,7], best_index stays 0. Support: write both compared values. Stretch: explain why a one-element list makes no loop-body passes but still has maximum index 0.","table":[["Current index","Comparison","Best index after"],["Start","Use first stored value","0"],["1","9 > 4","Complete"],["2","9 > 9","Complete"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Negative values and ties","strategy":"Guided variation and all-student checks","notes":"For[-8,-3], first maximum index 1/value-3. A guessed maximum value 0 is wrong because 0 is not present; best_index 0 correctly refers to real value-8. For[4,9,9], > retains first index 1 while >= updates to last index 2. Ask learners to commit predictions then explain the equality check. Support: label index/value separately.","body":["Predict the first maximum position in [-8, -3].","Why could a guessed maximum value of 0 fail?","For [4, 9, 9], compare using > and using >=.","Which comparison keeps the first occurrence?"],"minutes":10},{"title":"Adapting the search for a minimum","strategy":"Independent coding with optional scaffolds","notes":"Change > to \u003c and keep non-empty assumption. For[-2,4,-5,-5], first minimum index 2/value-5. For[7], index 0/value 7. Learners code and trace independently then compare evidence in pairs. Strict \u003c preserves first equal minimum, \u003c= gives last. Support: supply header and index loop. Stretch: implement max_index as a Week 5 function returning an index for non-empty input.","body":["Adapt the algorithm to find the first minimum.","Predict for [-2, 4, -5, -5] and [7].","Implement and compare expected with actual.","Explain your tie rule."],"minutes":12},{"title":"What about an empty list?","strategy":"Hinge question and error analysis","notes":"Correct B: no maximum position exists. Accessing values[0] on[] raises IndexError. Check len(values)==0 first, reporting no maximum, and use the algorithm only in the non-empty branch. If several choose A, ask what element index 0 would refer to. A total of 0 for an empty list does not imply maximum index 0.","body":["values = []","What is its maximum position?","A: 0     B: no maximum position exists","Choose alone. What must happen before reading values[0]?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: index, value and tie rule","strategy":"Independent exit ticket","notes":"For[-4,-1,-1], first maximum index 1/value-1; >= would retain 2. First minimum is index 0/value-4. Empty list needs a guard before accessing index 0. Collect individual explanations to identify tie-rule, sign or index confusion before the temperature task.","body":["Find the first maximum in [-4, -1, -1].","Give both its index and value.","Which comparison keeps the first maximum?","State the empty-list check needed before index 0."],"minutes":5}]},{"id":"u1-w06-l04","lesson":4,"title":"The temperature list task","chapter":"arrays","prepare":"Python editor and blank test log. Load exactly four suitable integer temperatures per run.","slides":[{"title":"The temperature list task","strategy":"Learning intention and transfer","notes":"Success means load four temperatures, display indices/values, find first minimum without min and defend test coverage. This is formative chapter transfer practice. Exact four-input contract guarantees non-empty data before the minimum search.","body":["Unit 1 · Week 6 · Lesson 4","Load temperatures and find the first minimum position."],"minutes":2},{"title":"Retrieval: a first occurrence","strategy":"Individual retrieval and peer explanation","notes":"For[-2,4,-5,-5], minimum value-5 at indices 2 and 3, first index 2. Strict \u003c keeps the earlier equal value. Ask learners to retrieve individually then compare. Revisit position versus value if they answer only-5.","body":["For [-2, 4, -5, -5], identify the minimum.","Give its first index and the other equal index.","Which comparison preserves the first minimum?","Compare your explanation with a partner."],"minutes":5},{"title":"Loading and displaying four readings","strategy":"Teacher modelling and guided prediction","notes":"Trace input-2,4,-5,-5 into the list. The four accepted integer inputs guarantee length 4 and indices 0..3. Display is a separate traversal after loading. print pairs 0 -2,1 4,2 -5,3 -5. Input validation is outside this core task. Ask learners to mark the two distinct loops.","body":["Predict the four index/value pairs.","Why do loading and display use separate traversals?"],"code":"temperatures = []\nfor index in range(4):\n    temperatures.append(int(input(\"Temperature: \")))\nfor index in range(len(temperatures)):\n    print(index, temperatures[index])","minutes":6},{"title":"Tests before the minimum search","strategy":"Independent prediction and pair comparison","notes":"Expected first minimum indices/values: [-2,4,-5,-5] ->2/-5; [6,3,8,9] ->1/3; [2,2,2,2] ->0/2. Learners predict from requirements before running. Last tied minimum would be 3 in first case, so this catches \u003c=. Support: label indices on paper. Stretch: choose a four-value case whose minimum occurs last, e.g.[3,2,1,0] ->3/0.","table":[["Four temperatures","First minimum index","Minimum value"],["-2, 4, -5, -5","Predict first","Predict first"],["6, 3, 8, 9","Predict first","Predict first"],["2, 2, 2, 2","Predict first","Predict first"]],"minutes":10},{"title":"Finding the first minimum","strategy":"Teacher modelling with faded support","notes":"This fragment follows the four-input loading code. Start lowest_index 0 at a real element. Strict \u003c changes only for a smaller temperature. For main test, trace best indices 0,0,2,2. Final output 2 -5. If reusing this algorithm for a variable-length list, check empty input before index 0. Support: retain index/value trace columns.","body":["Use this after loading four temperatures.","Trace the repeated minimum before running."],"code":"lowest_index = 0\nfor index in range(1, len(temperatures)):\n    if temperatures[index] \u003c temperatures[lowest_index]:\n        lowest_index = index\nprint(lowest_index, temperatures[lowest_index])","minutes":10},{"title":"Your program and test record","strategy":"Independent implementation and peer feedback","notes":"Learners combine loading, display and minimum search without using min. Run all predicted four-reading cases and preserve expected results. Partner challenges a tie-rule or index explanation after individual coding. Support: provide a blank loop skeleton. Stretch: separately test a reusable search with a one-element list and an empty-list guard, distinguishing those from the exactly-four-input interface.","body":["Combine loading, display and first-minimum search.","Run each predicted test set without using min.","Record any fault, correction and rerun.","Ask a partner to challenge one index or tie-rule decision."],"minutes":12},{"title":"Index 2 or the third reading?","strategy":"All-student hinge question","notes":"Correct B: index 2 is the third reading for Python zero-based indexing. In[-2,4,-5,-5], first minimum is index 2/value-5. Displaying human position lowest_index+1 is fine, but keep zero-based index internally for access. If several choose A, label indices 0..3 above item numbers 1..4.","body":["The first minimum has Python index 2.","It is A: the second reading     B: the third reading","Choose alone and explain.","How could you display a human-friendly reading number?"],"minutes":5},{"title":"Exit ticket: defend the evidence","strategy":"Independent exit ticket and reflection","notes":"For[3,1,1,4], first minimum index 1/value 1. Strict \u003c keeps it, while \u003c= returns 2. Equal values test the tie rule and negative values expose an unsafe guessed best value 0. Fixed four inputs ensure non-empty data; reusable search needs a guard. Collect an individual explanation before Week 7 files and reliable input.","body":["For [3, 1, 1, 4], give first minimum index and value.","Explain which comparison your program uses.","Name a test that checks repeated equal values.","Why is the four-input list safe to inspect at index 0?"],"minutes":5}]}]};
